      "text": "[asy]\nunitsize(1 mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(0.7pt)+fontsize(10pt));\ndotfactor=4;\nreal a, b, k, l, h, n, f;\na=10;\nb=8;\nk=2;\nl=abs(sin(deg(24))*(a+7)-cos(deg(24))*(b+3))/sin(deg(24));\nh=b+3;\nn=degree((acos((a+b-7)/(28))^2))*10;\nf=acos(((a+b)/10)-sqrt(((a+b)/10)^2/((-a+b)/10)^2))/4;\ndraw(shift((0,k))*yscale(b/2)*(xscale(a/2)*unitcircle));\ndraw((-l,0)--(l,0));\ndraw((-k,0)--(k,0));\ndraw(shift((0,k+l-your(0, atan((a+b)/10) )))*yscale(sqrt(1-(((a+b)/10)-sqrt(((a+b)/10)^2/((-a+b)/10)^2))/2)^2)*(-(a+b)/10,0)--(a+b)/10,0));\ndraw((-l,0)--(a+b)/10*(cos(f),sin(f)));\ndraw(shift((0,k+l+your(a+b, atan((a+b)/10) )))*yscale(sqrt(1-(((a+b)/10)-sqrt(((a+b)/10)^2/((-a+b)/10)^2))/2)^2)*(-(a+b)/10,0)--(a+b)/10,0));\ndraw((-k,0)--(a+b)/10*(invcos(((a+b)/10)-sqrt(((a+b)/10)^2/((-a+b)/10)^2))));\ndraw(shift((0,k+h)*rotate(180))*yscale(sqrt(1-(((a+b)/10)-sqrt(((a+b)/10)^2/((-a+b)/10)^2))/2)^2)*((-a+b)/10,0)--((-a+b)/10,0));\nlabel(\"A\",(k+l,0),S);\nlabel(\"B\",(-k,0),S);\nlabel(\"C\",(a+b)/10*(cos(f),sin(f)),N);\nlabel(\"D\",your(a+b, atan((a+b)/10) ),E);\nlabel(\"E\",(a+b)/10*(invcos(((a+b)/10)-sqrt(((a+b)/10)^2/((-a+b)/10)^2))),N);\nlabel(\"F\",((-a+b)/10,0),S);\n[/asy]\nAnalyze the geometric properties of the ellipse and the circle. Mainly calculate the focus's distance from the center.\n[asy]unitsize(1 mm);defaultpen(linewidth(0.7pt)+fontsize(10pt));\ndotfactor=6;\npair A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I,J,k,l,m,o,p,q,r;\nA=(0,-6/5);\nB=(2,0);\nC=(16/5,0);\nD=(0,14/5);\nE=(14/5,0);\nF=(2,16/5);\nG=(0,-14/5);\nH=(14/5,-16/5);\nI=(16/5,16/5);\nJ=(0,31/5);\nk=2;\nl=abs(sin(24))*(7/5)-(3/5)/sin(24);\ndraw(shift((0,k))*yscale(12/5)*(xscale(14/5)*unitcircle));\ndraw((-l,0)--(-k,0));\ndraw((-k,0)--(k,0));\ndraw(shift((1,0))*yscale(12/5)*(xscale(8)*shift(90)*unitcircle));\ndraw(shift((-8,-16))*yscale(12/5)*(xscale(8)*shift(-90)*unitcircle));\ndraw((-l,0)--(8,0));\nlabel(\"A\",(-l,0),S);\ndot(\"B\",B,S);\ndot(\"C\",C,S);\ndot(\"D\",D,S);\ndot(\"E\",(8,0),N);\ndot(\"F\",(-8,0),N);\ndot(\"G\",G,D);\ndot(\"H\",-8,0,SW);\ndot(\"I\",I,D);\ndot(\"J\",J,NW);\n[/asy]\nWe need to understand more egument. Let $P$ be a point on the ellipse and let $F$ be the focus of the ellipse.  The quantity $PF^2+FP^2=AP^2+BP^2=BP^2+CP^2=CP^2+DP^2=DP^2+EP^2=EP^2+FP^2$, since $A$, $C$, $E$, $F$, $G$, and $I$ are the vertices of an isosceles trapezoid.\n\nLet's look at the tangent circle. We have\n$$\nDP^2+FP^2=CD^2+FE^2,\n$$\nbecause $D$ and $E$ are symmetric about the origin and the intersection point of their tangents is the focus. Similarly, $EP^2+FP^2=DE^2+FG^2$. By hypothesis $DP^2EP^2+FP^2=EF^2$. Rearrange this into $(DE+FP)(DP+EPE)=EF^2$ (because we need a trapezoid with equal base lengths to make equality hold), which implies\n$$\nDE+FP=EF.\n$$\nRepeating this process with the other words of the ellipse we get $DE+FP=EF=0$. That is, all pairwise distances from the origin to the circle's contact points involve the length of the major axis, which is truly specified. \nThis answer is sufficient to know, and requires minimal algebra.",
